  "id": "hot_flood_npl_corridor",
  "stac_version": "1.1.0",
  "description": "OpenStreetMap and Overture Maps vector layers for the full Bhote Koshi and Trishuli\nriver corridor in Bagmati and Gandaki Provinces, Nepal, for the 2026 flood response.\n\nThe area of interest is a 1 km buffer around the river centrelines, running from\nRasuwagadhi on the China border to the Narayani confluence at Devghat in Chitawan, across\nRasuwa, Nuwakot, Dhading, Gorkha, Tanahu and Chitawan. It merges the original partner\nshapefile for the upper reach with OCHA's HPCS corridor downstream.\n\nThis is the wide planning view, the whole corridor under assessment rather than the\nground that flooded. For data limited to the flooded area, use [Nepal Flood 2026 Flood\nAffected Area](https://data.humdata.org/dataset/hot_flood_npl), which clips every layer\nto the observed inundation footprint of 27 August 2026 and a 200 m margin around it.\n\nEvery layer holds what is standing and what has been lost, so buildings and roads do not\nvanish from a download once mappers record them as destroyed. `status` says Standing,\nDamaged or Destroyed, and the Destroyed and Damaged Features layer gathers everything\nmarked destroyed or damaged in one place.\n\nLayers are rebuilt straight from OpenStreetMap each day, so they carry volunteer work as\nit lands. Buildings, schools, health facilities and points of interest also come in an\nOverture version, which fuses Microsoft, Google and OpenStreetMap and reaches ground\nvolunteers have not mapped yet. Overture follows its own release cycle and will not show\nmapping done during this response, so for damage work prefer the OpenStreetMap layers\nand use Overture where their coverage is thin.\n\nSubscribe to the dataset for updates as mapping progresses.\n\n- Source: [OpenStreetMap](https://www.openstreetmap.org/), [Overture Maps](https://overturemaps.org/)\n- Part of HOT disaster response exports on HDX by [HOT](https://www.hotosm.org/)\n",
